      "message": "Duplicate fetches for the same device can\u0027t be scheduled, so I documented the invariant rather than adding a lock for a case that can\u0027t occur.\n\nConversationRepository::fetch() has exactly one caller, conversation.cpp:2515, reached only from Conversation::Impl::pull(deviceId). Conversation::pull() (conversation.cpp:2456) uses fetchingRemotes_ as the in-flight marker itself: it is keyed by device, a worker is spawned only when emplace() reports the entry did not already exist, and only that worker erases the entry, as it exits. Both sides run under pullcbsMtx_, so there is at most one pull(deviceId) worker, hence at most one fetch(deviceId), at a time. A request for a device already being fetched is appended to that device\u0027s deque and drained by the running worker instead of starting a second fetch.\n\nFetches for different devices do still overlap, which is what the FETCH_HEAD change is for: refs/remotes/\u003cdevice\u003e and its reflog are per device, so those don\u0027t collide, but FETCH_HEAD was repository-wide and would have handed the second fetch a GIT_ELOCKED. Same reason git_remote_create() moved back under opMtx_ - it rewrites .git/config, which is also repository-wide. Neither waits on the network, so the lock still doesn\u0027t span the peer wait.\n\nThe comment at the unlock point now spells this out, and the commit message records it, so a future second caller of fetch() has the constraint in front of it.",
